course: reflection
lesson: 10
title: Laughed at: photos, scores, nicknames and the spotlight
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Being laughed at can happen in a second, on a phone screen or out loud in class, and then live for days in your head, getting bigger every time you check or replay it.
explain: Someone posted a photo of Ollie mid-sneeze in the class group chat with a mean caption, and thirty laughing faces appeared in five minutes; Ollie lay in bed refreshing it till midnight: "Even Tom put a laughing face. Tom's meant to be my mate."
explain: A laughing emoji takes one second, and most people added it without thinking about him at all; that does not make it okay, but it means the "whole class hates me" story in his head was much bigger than the truth, and every refresh fed the hurt.
explain: What helped was taking screenshots, showing Mum, the teacher getting the photo removed, Tom saying sorry, and muting the chat at night; posting someone to laugh at them is bullying, and it is the poster who did wrong, not the one in the photo.
explain: The disaster we imagine is usually much bigger than what really happens next, and when it truly is unfair, like being blamed for someone else's food fight or having a private photo shared, telling a grown-up fast is not grassing, it is getting it fixed.
fact: Posting a photo of someone to laugh at them, and "piling on" (everyone joining in), is a form of bullying; saving screenshots as proof and telling a grown-up is the right move.
fact: Checking a hurtful post again and again feeds the hurt; muting a chat at night can help.
fact: Reading test scores out loud turns a private mark into a public label; a low score shows what you haven't learned yet, not how clever you are.
fact: Calling someone a nasty name for "stupid" is bullying; such words stick because they come at a weak moment, but they are not the truth.
fact: Lots of children and plenty of grown-ups still have a comfort toy; the disaster we imagine after teasing is usually much bigger than what really happens.
fact: Fainting can happen when you are hot, hungry or stand still too long; it is the body, not a weakness, and eating breakfast and drinking water help.
fact: Voices crack sometimes, more as children grow; it happens to everyone, and avoiding reading out loud makes the fear grow.
fact: Not having much money is nothing to be ashamed of; many families are in the same boat ("skint" and "hard up" mean short of money), and a teacher should not share family money matters in front of a class.
fact: Doing dares to be liked often backfires: people remember the silly thing, not the bravery, and a bigger dare usually digs the hole deeper.
fact: Photos of a child without clothes, even baby photos, should never be shared; consent means saying yes before something happens, and telling a grown-up fast is right.
fact: Laughter can be friendly, not mean; people often like you more if you can laugh at yourself.
fact: Being told off in public hurts more than in private, and being wrongly blamed adds a feeling of injustice that lasts after others forget.
fact: Embarrassment changes how we see things: people who were worried about us can look, in our memory, as if they were laughing.
exercise: Ollie's sneeze photo got thirty laughing faces. Why might so many kids have added one? | A laughing emoji takes a second and many added it without thinking | Every single one of them had planned for weeks to hurt him and make him cry | They all secretly hated Ollie and wanted him gone | Laughing faces are the only reaction the chat allows
exercise: What helped Ollie with the group chat photo? | Screenshots, telling Mum, removal and a muted chat | Refreshing the chat until midnight to see who laughed | Posting a worse photo of the kid who started it | Leaving the photo up so people got bored of it
exercise: Ruby's teacher read out "four out of twenty" and someone whispered a nasty name meaning stupid. What does a low score really show? | What she hasn't learned yet, not how clever she is | That she is the least clever person in the class forever | That she should stop trying at maths altogether now | That the teacher thinks she is lazy and rude in class
exercise: What helped Ruby with maths? | A tutor at lunchtime and a chart showing her own progress | Calling herself dumb every time she got stuck, to push herself harder | Avoiding maths lessons by pretending to be ill | Copying answers so the scores looked better
exercise: The boys found Ethan's teddy, Mr Bun, at a sleepover. On Monday only one boy mentioned it. What does this show? | The disaster we imagine is often much bigger than what really happens | The boys were quietly planning something even worse for later in the term | Ethan was lucky; usually the whole school finds out | Comfort toys are always found out sooner or later
exercise: Grace fainted in assembly in front of the whole school. Why do people faint? | Often from being hot, hungry or standing still too long | Because they are weak and need to try harder | Because something embarrassing is about to happen to them in public | Only because of a serious illness
exercise: After fainting, Grace thought everyone was laughing, but they asked if she was okay. What happened in her mind? | Embarrassment made worried faces look like laughing ones | Her friends were really laughing at her and only pretending to worry | Fainting changes your eyesight for a whole week | She guessed right; worried people always laugh
exercise: Noah's voice cracked while reading aloud and a boy copied the squeak. Noah fakes a sore throat for two weeks. What happened when he tried again? | He read one short paragraph and nobody laughed | Everyone laughed even harder than they had before | His voice cracked worse and worse every single time | The teacher banned him from reading aloud for good
exercise: The teacher said in front of everyone that Amira wasn't coming on the trip, and everyone guessed it was money. What is true? | Being short of money is no shame, nor a class matter | Amira's family should be ashamed of not paying | Amira must have done something wrong to miss the trip | Families short of money are rare, so she is unusual
exercise: Finn ate a worm for a dare to look tough and became "Worm Boy". What did NOT help? | Planning an even bigger dare to fix it | Laughing it off with his mates and moving on | Saying "nah" to the very next dare he got | Waiting quietly for the nickname to fade away
exercise: Isla's cousin shared a baby bath photo of her with her class. What is true? | Photos of a child with no clothes must never be shared | It's fine because she was only a tiny baby in the photo, so nobody minds | She should get revenge by sharing a photo of him | It's only a problem if someone laughs at it
exercise: Archie fell off his skateboard showing off and a girl laughed. Then she asked, "That was funny, you okay?" What does this show? | Laughter can be friendly, not mean | She was secretly being cruel the whole time | Girls always laugh at boys who fall | He should never skate in front of anyone again
exercise: The head teacher shouted at Leah in the dinner hall for throwing food, but it was the boy next to her. Why did it hurt so much? | Public blame plus being wrongly blamed feels unfair | Being told off only ever hurts when you really are guilty | Head teachers are not allowed to shout at all | She was secretly guilty and felt bad about it
exercise: What helped Leah? | Her mum talking to the school, and the head saying sorry | Skipping lunch in the hall for weeks | Throwing food at the boy in the dinner hall the next day to get him back | Telling everyone the head teacher hates her
test: Ollie's mate says, "just keep checking the chat so you know who's laughing." What's a smarter idea, and why? | Mute it and screenshot it; checking feeds the hurt | Check every five minutes, because knowing makes it hurt less | Reply with a laughing face so it looks like he doesn't care | Leave the chat and never tell anyone about it
test: Tom laughed at Ollie's photo but later said sorry. Ollie still feels a sting when Tom laughs. Which is the wisest view? | One thoughtless click hurt; trust can come back slowly after a real sorry | Tom must have hated him all along, so the friendship is over | The sting means Ollie is too sensitive and should toughen up | Tom's sorry didn't count because it came later
test: Ruby scored 4/20 in one test and 11/20 a month later. A kid says, "she was bottom, so she's just not clever." What does the change show? | She's learning; a score shows what she knows so far | Nothing; once you're bottom of the class you stay bottom | The second test must have been much easier than the first | She must have copied someone else's answers that time
test: Ethan laughed along at the sleepover and later lied that the teddy was his sister's. Why did the lie make things harder? | He then had to keep the story up, which made him more anxious | Lies always get found out within one hour | His sister would get into trouble at school | There was no problem at all; the lie fixed everything and nobody asked again
test: Grace's nan from Newcastle: "Ee, pet, ye just keeled ower, nowt to be ashamed of." ("Keeled over" means fell down; "nowt" means nothing.) What helped Grace not faint again? | Breakfast, water and standing at the end of the row for a while | Standing perfectly still for longer to toughen up | Skipping breakfast so she felt lighter | Never going to assembly again
test: What would happen if Noah kept saying "sore throat" to avoid reading for the whole year? | His fear of reading out loud would probably grow | His voice would simply stop cracking all by itself | Everyone would soon forget he had ever read out loud | His reading aloud would become perfect without practice
test: Amira made up a story about a holiday so nobody would know her family was skint. Why might that not help? | She'd have to keep the story up, and there's nothing to be ashamed of | Made-up stories always get found out on the same day | Other families would then have to lie too | Teachers check every holiday story with parents
test: Finn's mate, Gen Alpha: "bro, eat a spider next, that's mad clout." ("Clout" means being noticed.) What's the smart move? | Say "nah"; a bigger dare usually digs the hole deeper | Do it, because a bigger dare always wipes out a smaller one | Do it, but only if more people are watching | Dare his mate to do it first, then copy
test: Isla's cousin says, "it's just a baby pic, chill." Isla's furious. Who's right? | Isla; such photos must never be shared, so tell an adult | Her cousin; baby photos don't count at all, whoever is in them | Her cousin; she should chill and laugh along | Neither; nobody can stop photos being shared
test: In a game, every time a player falls, other players spam laughing emotes. Archie says, "same as the skate park, everyone's laughing AT you." What's the clever reply? | Some laughter is friendly; check, like the girl did | All laughter is always mean, in games and in real life | Laughter is always friendly, even when it's meant to hurt | You can never tell, so assume everyone hates you
test: Leah's mum, Belfast: "They fit you up, love, I'm away up to that school." ("Fit up" means blamed for something you didn't do.) Why does the unfairness last after others forget? | Being wrongly blamed leaves a lasting sense of injustice | Because Leah secretly did throw some food | Because everyone at school will remember it and talk about it forever | It doesn't last; unfairness is forgotten fastest
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the publication of a photograph of a pupil for the purpose of ridicule? | It constitutes a form of bullying for which the publisher is responsible | It constitutes a harmless jest for which no person is responsible | It is the fault of the person photographed for having sneezed | It is acceptable provided that the number of reactions is small
exam: It is given that a pupil obtained four marks out of twenty. Determine which inference is consistent with the established facts. | The score indicates material not yet learned rather than fixed ability | The score establishes that the pupil is permanently of low ability | The score demonstrates that further effort would be futile | The score indicates that the assessment was improperly conducted
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a child subjected to teasing commonly anticipates consequences more severe than those which ensue. | Embarrassment enlarges the imagined disaster beyond what actually occurs | Teasing invariably escalates to the whole school within a day | Children possess an accurate knowledge of the intentions of others | The consequences are customarily concealed by the teasers
exam: Ascertain which response is consistent with the facts concerning a pupil who undertakes a dare in order to secure popularity. | Such dares commonly fail, and a larger dare tends to aggravate the position | Such dares reliably secure lasting admiration among peers | A larger subsequent dare invariably erases the memory of the first | Such dares are of no consequence whatsoever to one's reputation
